nodecode-linear-digest is a rewrite of TakeshiTGAL's TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-linear-digest:

  https://github.com/TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-linear-digest
  commit cb8cac04dbe3839bf75557757df56d3f3c31e3e9
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 TakeshiTGAL (LICENSE)

The plugin was a Hermes tool, a skill and a scheduled no-agent script over one
GraphQL query: the open Linear issues assigned to one personal API key, in four
groups -- overdue, due today, not updated for N days, and the rest -- with the
headings counted, the issue lines tab-separated, at most thirty of them shown,
a title forced onto one line, a URL kept only when it is https on linear.app,
and up to ten pages of fifty read per run. This rewrite keeps that query and
that list: the same document, the same four groups and order, the same caps and
the same words for an unreadable due date or a refused URL, answered as the
linear_digest tool, printed by /linear-digest, and explained on (help
:linear-digest). It speaks the same GraphQL endpoint directly through nlk:http,
with the key read from the cell's section or from LINEAR_API_KEY and sent as
the Authorization value with no Bearer prefix, to api.linear.app and nowhere
else. Nothing is written to Linear in either.

What was left out: the schedule, unschedule and status subcommands and the
morning script they wrote, because a Nodecode cell is started by the gateway and
a prompt that runs on a clock is the cron cell's job; the profile-scoped secret
reader, the settings file under the plugin's own directory and the loopback
rehearsal variable, which existed for that script's own tests; the registered
skill, whose guidance is now the manual topic the model reads every turn; and
the timezone re-binding the script did for a child process, since the list is
built in this image on the clock the cell already reads. Nothing of the original
is vendored: there was no code to reuse, only the wire it spoke and the rules
it enforced.
